WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.563
Look at Earth from space and you see why it is called the blue planet.

00:00:03.793 --> 00:00:06.822
Water covers about seventy-one percent of its surface.

00:00:07.052 --> 00:00:11.383
It occurs in connected oceans and seas, and in smaller rivers and lakes.

00:00:11.613 --> 00:00:17.629
These water bodies are not interchangeable: their size, shape, location and kind of water differ.

00:00:18.379 --> 00:00:21.728
Oceans contain about ninety-seven percent of Earth's water.

00:00:21.958 --> 00:00:28.678
Most ocean water is salty, so a large amount of water on the planet does not mean unlimited drinking water.

00:00:28.908 --> 00:00:36.759
Fresh water in rivers and lakes supports people, animals, farming and many habitats, but it must be protected from pollution.

00:00:37.509 --> 00:00:40.111
This chapter asks you to do more than recite names.

00:00:40.341 --> 00:00:48.704
Locate each water body on a map, describe how it differs from others, and connect water with trade, climate, civilization and daily life.

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:54.587
We begin with the five oceans and follow their edges toward seas, bays, gulfs and straits.

00:00:56.087 --> 00:01:01.719
The five named oceans are the Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Arctic and Southern Oceans.

00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:05.704
On a globe they connect, yet their names help us locate regions.

00:01:05.934 --> 00:01:14.446
Start with the Pacific on both sides of a flat world map: it lies between Asia and Australia on one side and the Americas on the other.

00:01:15.196 --> 00:01:18.695
The Atlantic separates the Americas from Europe and Africa.

00:01:18.925 --> 00:01:23.725
The Indian Ocean lies south of Asia, east of Africa and west of Australia.

00:01:23.955 --> 00:01:25.811
The Arctic surrounds the North Pole.

00:01:26.041 --> 00:01:28.601
The Southern Ocean circles Antarctica.

00:01:28.831 --> 00:01:33.652
Point to each one as I speak, rather than trying to memorize a list without positions.

00:01:34.402 --> 00:01:41.485
A flat map can trick you: the Pacific appears cut into two pieces at its left and right edges, though it is one ocean.

00:01:41.715 --> 00:01:44.253
Trace the water around the globe with your finger.

00:01:44.483 --> 00:01:50.691
Notice how the Southern Ocean joins the southern parts of the Pacific, Atlantic and Indian Oceans.

00:01:51.441 --> 00:01:55.175
Which ocean lies between the Americas and Europe and Africa?

00:02:03.405 --> 00:02:04.770
The Atlantic Ocean.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.904
Find the Americas on one side and Europe and Africa on the other.

00:02:09.884 --> 00:02:12.081
The Pacific is the largest ocean.

00:02:12.311 --> 00:02:16.834
The chapter describes it as almost circular and covering over one-third of the globe.

00:02:17.064 --> 00:02:22.525
On the map, its enormous width is easier to see if you remember that the two map edges meet.

00:02:22.755 --> 00:02:26.830
Its basin contains many islands as well as deep ocean floor.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:34.449
Around much of its margin, moving tectonic plates are associated with volcanoes, earthquakes and mountain belts.

00:02:34.679 --> 00:02:37.623
This active zone is often called the Ring of Fire.

00:02:37.853 --> 00:02:41.544
The name is a clue to location, not a literal ring of flames.

00:02:41.774 --> 00:02:44.761
Relate it to the plate movements from your landforms chapter.

00:02:45.511 --> 00:02:53.916
The Mariana Trench, near the western Pacific, contains the deepest known point in the ocean, close to eleven kilometres below sea level.

00:02:54.146 --> 00:02:57.239
A trench is a long, narrow depression on the seabed.

00:02:57.469 --> 00:03:01.352
The chapter also names the Philippine, Japan and Kuril trenches.

00:03:01.582 --> 00:03:05.443
Remember the contrast: a vast surface above a very deep floor.

00:03:06.943 --> 00:03:09.610
The Atlantic is the second largest ocean.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.571
The chapter likens its outline to the letter S.

00:03:12.801 --> 00:03:19.286
Europe and Africa lie along its eastern side; North and South America lie along its western side.

00:03:19.516 --> 00:03:24.209
Find those continents first, then the Atlantic's shape becomes easier to understand.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:28.799
Many long-established shipping routes link ports around the Atlantic.

00:03:29.029 --> 00:03:33.403
The textbook describes it as the busiest ocean for trade and transport.

00:03:33.633 --> 00:03:37.643
Its coastline has numerous bays, gulfs and natural harbour sites.

00:03:37.873 --> 00:03:43.719
That helps explain why people have crossed it for trade, travel and exploration over many centuries.

00:03:44.469 --> 00:03:50.335
Beneath the middle of the Atlantic runs the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, a long submerged mountain system.

00:03:50.565 --> 00:03:55.707
Parts rise above sea level to form islands; Iceland is a volcanic example.

00:03:55.937 --> 00:03:59.563
A ridge is raised seabed, while a trench is a deep depression.

00:03:59.793 --> 00:04:03.569
Compare this cross-section with the Pacific trench in the previous scene.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:09.077
Is the Mid-Atlantic Ridge a mountain chain above the sea or mostly beneath it?

00:04:17.307 --> 00:04:19.056
It is mostly beneath the sea.

00:04:19.286 --> 00:04:22.358
Some peaks emerge as islands, including Iceland.

00:04:23.338 --> 00:04:25.983
The Indian Ocean is roughly triangular.

00:04:26.213 --> 00:04:32.549
Asia closes its northern side, Africa borders it to the west, and Australia lies toward the east.

00:04:32.779 --> 00:04:35.403
Most of its area is in the Southern Hemisphere.

00:04:35.633 --> 00:04:41.607
The chapter notes that India lies at its northern end, which is why this ocean carries the country's name.

00:04:42.357 --> 00:04:44.533
Look closely at the water beside India.

00:04:44.763 --> 00:04:49.221
The Arabian Sea is west of the peninsula; the Bay of Bengal is east.

00:04:49.451 --> 00:04:51.371
Both connect to the Indian Ocean.

00:04:51.601 --> 00:04:59.452
Locating them helps you place India on a world map and gives a simple way to remember the peninsula's three water-facing sides.

00:05:00.202 --> 00:05:03.999
The Indian Ocean links coasts of Africa, Asia and Australia.

00:05:04.229 --> 00:05:07.621
Sea routes across it have carried people, goods and ideas.

00:05:07.851 --> 00:05:12.267
The ocean also supplies moisture that later falls as rain over land.

00:05:12.497 --> 00:05:19.260
Keep the geography concrete: name the bordering continents and then point to the Arabian Sea and Bay of Bengal.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:26.797
The Arctic Ocean lies north of North America, Europe and Asia, mostly within the Arctic Circle.

00:05:27.027 --> 00:05:29.929
It is the smallest and shallowest of the five oceans.

00:05:30.159 --> 00:05:36.089
Sea ice covers much of it for long periods, although that cover changes with the seasons and climate.

00:05:36.319 --> 00:05:38.581
The North Pole is in an ocean region.

00:05:39.331 --> 00:05:43.939
The Southern Ocean, also called the Antarctic Ocean, surrounds Antarctica.

00:05:44.169 --> 00:05:49.033
It is where the southern waters of the Pacific, Atlantic and Indian Oceans meet.

00:05:49.263 --> 00:05:53.743
The South Pole, unlike the North Pole, sits on the land mass of Antarctica.

00:05:53.973 --> 00:05:57.941
That distinction is more useful than memorizing a rank by size.

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:00.611
Picture two polar diagrams.

00:06:00.841 --> 00:06:04.190
At the top of the world, draw ocean with continents around it.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.577
At the bottom, draw Antarctica with ocean around it.

00:06:07.807 --> 00:06:10.367
That one reversal stops a common mix-up.

00:06:10.597 --> 00:06:14.309
Both are cold, but the positions of land and sea are different.

00:06:15.059 --> 00:06:20.243
Which polar ocean surrounds a continent, and which polar ocean is bordered by continents?

00:06:28.473 --> 00:06:31.012
The Southern Ocean surrounds Antarctica.

00:06:31.242 --> 00:06:35.125
The Arctic Ocean is bordered by North America, Europe and Asia.

00:06:36.105 --> 00:06:41.459
Oceans support an enormous variety of aquatic life and provide food for many people.

00:06:41.689 --> 00:06:44.633
They also contain mineral and energy resources.

00:06:44.863 --> 00:06:49.429
People use their coastlines and sea routes for travel and international trade.

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:55.952
These benefits depend on healthy water and careful use, because damage can spread far beyond one shore.

00:06:56.702 --> 00:06:58.430
Oceans help shape climate.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:02.201
Water evaporates from the sea, adding water vapour to the air.

00:07:02.431 --> 00:07:05.226
That vapour can later condense and fall as rain.

00:07:05.456 --> 00:07:09.765
Large water bodies also tend to moderate temperatures near coasts.

00:07:09.995 --> 00:07:16.353
Connect these ideas: ocean, evaporation, air movement, rainfall and living conditions on land.

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:19.236
Moving seawater carries energy too.

00:07:19.466 --> 00:07:27.018
Tides are the regular rise and fall of sea level, and in suitable places their movement can be used to generate electricity.

00:07:27.248 --> 00:07:35.760
When you explain importance, give different kinds of reasons: a food or habitat reason, a transport reason and a climate or energy reason.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:42.700
A sea is generally a smaller, shallower part of an ocean, often close to a continent's edge.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:55.175
On a world map, find the Caribbean Sea near Central America, the Bering Sea between Alaska and northeastern Asia, the Arabian Sea beside western India and the North Sea near northwestern Europe.

00:07:55.925 --> 00:08:02.923
The Mediterranean Sea lies between Europe and Africa and opens to the Atlantic through the Strait of Gibraltar.

00:08:03.153 --> 00:08:07.953
Seas have long provided routes for voyages and exchange between coastlines.

00:08:08.183 --> 00:08:15.777
Their locations help explain why particular regions came into contact, although maps alone cannot tell the full human story.

00:08:16.527 --> 00:08:22.181
Some large saltwater bodies have 'sea' in their names even though they are cut off from an open ocean.

00:08:22.411 --> 00:08:26.997
The chapter mentions the Caspian Sea, Black Sea and Aral Sea as inland seas.

00:08:27.227 --> 00:08:34.801
Focus on the map relationship: is the water at an ocean edge, enclosed by land, or joined by a narrow passage?

00:08:35.551 --> 00:08:39.796
Name one sea at the edge of an ocean and one inland sea from the chapter.

00:08:48.026 --> 00:08:53.978
For example, the Arabian Sea borders the Indian Ocean, and the Caspian Sea is an inland sea.

00:08:54.958 --> 00:08:58.841
A bay is a broad, curved inlet where water enters the coastline.

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:05.769
The Bay of Bengal east of India, Hudson Bay in North America and the Bay of Biscay near Europe are examples.

00:09:05.999 --> 00:09:12.079
Draw a coastline with a wide opening and a gentle curve: that picture helps more than the word alone.

00:09:12.829 --> 00:09:18.909
A gulf is commonly a more deeply indented and enclosed arm of the sea, often with a narrower opening.

00:09:19.139 --> 00:09:26.350
Examples in the chapter include the Persian Gulf, Gulf of Mexico and Gulf of Carpentaria north of Australia.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:33.407
These labels are conventional, so compare shapes as a guide rather than expecting a perfect rule for every name.

00:09:34.157 --> 00:09:39.021
To answer the textbook comparison, state that both are inlets of water into land.

00:09:39.251 --> 00:09:45.715
Then contrast a bay's broader opening with a gulf's usually deeper indentation and greater enclosure.

00:09:45.945 --> 00:09:51.769
Locate the Bay of Bengal and Persian Gulf on a map; examples anchor the shape in a real place.

00:09:53.269 --> 00:09:57.493
A strait is a narrow stretch of water connecting two larger water bodies.

00:09:57.723 --> 00:10:00.944
Think of a watery passage between two pieces of land.

00:10:01.174 --> 00:10:05.974
The Strait of Gibraltar connects the Mediterranean Sea with the Atlantic Ocean.

00:10:06.204 --> 00:10:10.535
Trace that short connection on a map near southern Spain and northern Morocco.

00:10:11.285 --> 00:10:15.359
Closer to India, the Palk Strait lies between India and Sri Lanka.

00:10:15.589 --> 00:10:20.944
The chapter's map shows it connecting the Bay of Bengal side with the Gulf of Mannar side.

00:10:21.174 --> 00:10:27.574
A strait can be an important route for ships, but its narrowness can also make navigation and access sensitive.

00:10:28.324 --> 00:10:38.820
End part one by comparing four shapes aloud: a sea is part of a larger ocean, a bay or gulf is an inlet along a coast, and a strait is a narrow connection.

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:40.693
Point to one example of each.

00:10:40.923 --> 00:10:46.533
Next, we move inland to lakes and rivers, then follow water into human life and pollution.

00:10:47.283 --> 00:10:51.571
What does a strait connect, and which strait lies between India and Sri Lanka?

00:10:59.801 --> 00:11:02.297
A strait connects two larger water bodies.

00:11:02.527 --> 00:11:05.493
The Palk Strait lies between India and Sri Lanka.
